In article <2uqvmm$80k@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>, ayman@uiatma.atmos.uiuc.edu (Ayman_Ghanem) writes:
|> Thanks for the suggestions posted in reply ro my question about allocating
|> more swap space.

|> ... does the system know that
|> the b partition is the swap space (regardless of what the partition size
|> will be), or is the size of the swap space written somewhere in one of the
|> system files?.

The system sizes the swap partitions as they come online.  The size is
set by the information in the partition table; it is not compiled into
the system.

Neither is the exact partition on which the system swaps.  If you want
to swap on the "d" partition for example and use "b" for something else, 
just edit /etc/fstab, change the "swap1" device to reflect that and reboot.
The system will come up and swap on the "d" partition.  Neat, eh?

					Frank


