In comp.unix.osf.osf1 you write:

>In article <werme.772464781@alingo.zk3.dec.com>,
>Eric Werme <werme@zk3.dec.com> wrote:
>> I'm not completely certain how the shutdown stuff really works, but
>> "shutdown -r" runs reboot *after* running rc0.d/K*.

>I hate like hell to contradict you, since you're at DEC, but I think
>you're wrong.

No problem.  Ask me something about NFS.  One thing I like to do with
the rc3 script is print the name of the script it is about to execute.
You might want to do the same with the rc0 script:

        # Kill procedures
        for f in /sbin/rc0.d/K*
        do
add --->	echo -------- $f
                if [ -s $f ]; then
                        /sbin/sh $f stop
                fi
        done

>*however*, we've had problems in the past where shutdown -r didn't properly
>run rc0.

I don't recall seeing that roll by in the bug reports.  You might want to
talk to Customer Support about it.

> In addition, examining shutdown with "strings" shows that it's
>calling "/sbin/reboot" directly.

Like I said ""shutdown -r" runs reboot...."

>(It also shows an amazing amount of stuff statically linked in the
> executable, including prompts from programs like "passwd". What
> gives?)

Well, everything in /sbin is linked statically.  Shutdown has to write
in the syslog file and includes the user's name.  That drags in code
that deals with /etc/passwd, and I'd guess one of the modules has a
routine that shutdown doesn't call, but does includes a call to the
routine that changes passwords.  We've never had the time to clean up
all those nits.  Look at "/usr"/sbin/shutdown for a variant that uses
the shared library - there's a lot less extra stuff.
