Next time I'm playing with one of the workstations I'll do that.

Short of that, though, I can't see any indication that shutdown -r runs any
rc scripts. There's nothing in inittab or rc0 to make rc0 run reboot. When
rc0 gets to the end it either calls "init S" or "halt -q", so shutdown can't
run it and then call reboot, and init can't run it and then call reboot,
and there's no other code to run the rc0 scripts anywhere that I can see.

I haven't called it in as a bug because I don't know what your *intent* is,
here. After all, the core is still the stock BSD code with /sbin/reboot
and /sbin/halt. I'm grateful that it has run levels at all.

Now that you've implied that you *do* want it to work right (you in the
collective sense, of course) I'll see about pushing it through channels.

How about adding a "-i" option to shutdown as well, so we can add custom
run levels (like standalone backup) more easily?
