There should already be a shell on your system that does this:/bin/ksh

If this isn't on your system, or you prefer public domain software, I
would suggest grabbing the latest version of either tcsh, or bash.

Tcsh is an extended version of csh that uses csh style initialization
file syntax, and can be used in place of csh with no differences,
except for command line editing (either emacs or vi style) & file name
completion.

Tcsh does some really cool things that I'm not aware of other shells
being able to do.  One is context sensitive filename completion.  Via
the command: 'complete cd p/1/d/', I can make tcsh ignore everything
but directories when I ask it to complete filename for the cd command.

Bash is put out by GNU and is descended from sh, (Born Again SH).  It
uses sh style initialization file syntax.  It also does filename
completion & command line editing.

Have fun,

