Try this sometime:

= cat > ~/.telnetrc
= default
= 	environ unexport USER
= ^D

	I like the feature. I use telnet to get my display variable
copied around, I don't like .rhosts files all over the place, so I
am prepared to type my password but don't want to have to type in too
many things.

	If your view that it is a pain in the arse because you have
different account names on different machines, then go ahead and make
entries for that host. Or only have entries for the hosts you do want
it for - but note that you'll have to re-export USER since the defaults
get rid of it, eg:

= cat > ~/.telnetrc
= default
=       environ unexport USER
= xxx.yyy.zzz
= 	environ define USER username
= 	environ export USER
= ^D

	I like it. If you don't, there are documented ways around it.
(Although DEC documentation doesn't refer to "default" in one's
.telnetrc file, it's pretty obvious when everything else of similar
vein does, such as .netrc for ftp. :-( ) I don't use a .telnetrc file
myself.

