In article <CrAFvH.3Ar@news.cern.ch>, julian@axcn01.cern.ch 
(Julian James Bunn) writes:
>
>Steve, would you care to estimate when we'll be seeing your F90 compiler
>for OpenVMS and Windows/NT ?

As soon as we possibly can; we're actively developing for those two
platforms right now.  If it was simply a matter of porting the compiler
we could be ready to enter field test in weeks; we've already got the
compiler running native on OpenVMS AXP.  The RTL is there (on both
platforms) already, as it's the same as used for DEC Fortran (which thus 
allows you to mix DEC Fortran 90 and DEC Fortran modules in the same 
application.)  For OpenVMS we've got to add compiler support for things
like library-based INCLUDE and indexed I/O.  We also have to make sure
that debugging support is there as well as various other details that will
push out the time a bit.  If it takes us more than six months, I'll
be disappointed.

I'd be interested to hear from OpenVMS users which OpenVMS-related
DEC Fortran features you could live without in an initial release of
DEC Fortran 90 for OpenVMS AXP (especially if you could continue to
use DEC Fortran for those parts of your application which needed them.)
I'd also want to know which OpenVMS compiler features you COULDN'T live 
without in an initial release.  (Examples: library-based INCLUDE,
DICTIONARY, SCA support, FORSYSDEF.TLB with F90 derived types instead
of STRUCTUREs, etc.)  Send me mail.

