Summary: wall in shutdown hangs on phantom ttys
Keywords: shutdown utmp wall init

In article <1994Jun15.144348.19298@govonca.gov.on.ca>,
Dick Abraham <abrahad@govonca.gov.on.ca> wrote:
>I have 3 DEC Alpha 3000/300's running OSF/1 ver 1.3  Frequently when I
>attempt a shutdown it runs giving me the appropriate messages and 
>disableing logins but does not shut the computer down.
>
>Anyone seen this or know what might be causing it.

We experienced the same problem. I believe it is a problem with a user,
who has logged out (or switched off his X Terminal), but is still 
registered somewhere in utmp.

If you check the logged in users with the "w" or "who" commands, you see him:

# w
username  p8  xterminal9  09:23  10days   -

If you check the processes you will not find any process associated to the
terminal line:

# ps -eftp8
USER       PID  PPID %CPU STARTED  TT       TIME COMMAND

The shutdown process attempts a "wall" to all connected ttys, which hangs at
the invalid tty. If you use wall under such circumstances, your terminal will
hang. You can see the hanging wall and shutdown processes with "ps -ef", but
cannot kill them.

WORKARAOUND:
Shut down the system without wall, just use "init 0" in such a case.

Q: Where come the invalid utmp entries from?

Maybe they sometimes stay in the system, when the nice little dxterm or mwm 
core dumps are created when ending a session. Is there a patch? 

Somewhere I read you should exit all DECterms before quitting a session.
Do you use X Window Terminals in your environment?


