In article <2ua2cq$kel@newpisgah.keene.edu>,
R. David Murray <rdmurray@keene.edu> wrote:
> How do you use xauth under osf 1.3?  I tried a simple script that does
> 
>  xauth extract - $DISPLAY | rsh $1 -l $name /usr/bin/X11/xauth merge -
> 
> where $1 is the target machine and $name is the target userid.  But when
> I do xauth list on the target machine, it shows the display name as (eg)

Well, it has nothing to do with osf/1 1.3 (it's generic X).

The X server has two Xcookies to access the display depending on how
you access it: there's a cookie for local connections (through unix:0
or :0) and another one for all other connections.

In the xauth listing, the local connection appears as
"machine.name/unix:0". So what you're doing is passing the local key,
which is useless since the remote machine has to use a "normal"
connection. This happens because you use "$DISPLAY" which generally
happens to be ":0" (and hence selects the cookie for local
connections).

Just use

	xauth extract - `hostname`:0 | ...

This will select the correct cookie for remote
connections. Alternatively, you can arrange to check that your DISPLAY
never uses ":0" but always the full machine name (this *may* slow down
X a little bit, but it's not sure), of course.


	Stefan


