In article <2ubec7Ebqk@uni-erlangen.de>,
Horst Luehrsen <Horst.Luehrsen@Informatik.Uni-Erlangen.DE> wrote:
> If we use shutdown -r to shutdown and reboot the machine, the machine shuts
> down really fast and I think, it doesn't execute the /sbin/rc2.d/K00ingres,
> but instead kills all processes.

> Am I right with this guess? Or did I put K00ingres in the wrong directory?

You're right.

You're being hosed by the BSD shutdown model. In BSD based systems it sends
a signal 15 to all procs, waits "a reasonable time", then sends a signal 9.
In System V based systems it sends a signal to init to change the run level,
and then init's responsible for running shutdown scripts.

DEC merges the two inits. For regular shutdown, it (or init, I'm not too clear)
runs the shutdown scripts. For shutdown -r it runs the command "reboot". What
it should do is set the run level to 6 and let init run reboot when it finishes
changing the run level. The stock OSF inittab doesn't even *have* a run level
six defined.

:-<

System V defines init levels 0 and 6 like this:

r0:056:wait:/etc/rc0  1> /dev/console 2>&1 </dev/console
sd:0:wait:/etc/uadmin 2 0 >/dev/console 2>&1 </dev/console
rb:6:wait:/etc/uadmin 2 1 >/dev/console 2>&1 </dev/console

It runs rc0 (which sees it's going from level 2 or 3 to level 6, and runs
rc0.d/K*) then does a uadmin 2 1, which does a reboot.

In OSF, you get this:

s0:0:wait:/sbin/rc0 off < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1

I'd suggest that DEC add a "reboot" option to /sbin/rc0:
that does:

#
# Reboot if it's booting time...
#
if [ "$ARGONE" = reboot ]; then
	/sbin/reboot
else
	... existing code ...
fi

then add:

s6:6:wait:/sbin/rc0 reboot < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1

So you'd use "init 6" to reboot cleanly, like in Ssystem V.
