This is right. The problem seems to be that OSF/1 seems to have a mish-mash of
functions defined for various OS's (BSD, SYSV, etc) and does not appear
uniform for one particular OS. tcsh isn't too bad. I'll make a patch (forgot
to keep the original files :-( ) sometime. Here's what worked for me:

in IMakefile:

# ifdef AlphaArchitecture
#  define ConfigH decosf1
#  if !HasGCC
#   if (OSMajorVersion == 2)                       /***** new *****/
#    define MyCflags -std1 -Olimit 2000 -Dosf1v2   /***** new *****/
#   else                                           /***** new *****/
#    define MyCflags -std1 -Olimit 2000            /***** new *****/
#   endif                                          /***** new *****/
#  endif
# endif

Then run xmkmf again.

In 'tc.os.h':

line 586: (around setpwent and endpwent)
# if (!defined(fps500) && !defined(apollo) && !defined(__lucid)) && \
                                                            !defined(osf1v2)
                                                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
line 625: (around memmove and bcopy)
#if (defined(BSD) && !defined(__386BSD__) && !defined(osf1v2)) || \
                                                                defined(SUNOS4)
    ^                                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

line 645: (__osf__ && __alpha block)
extern int gethostname __P((char *, int));
#ifdef osf1v2						/***** new *****/
extern char *sbrk __P((ssize_t));                       /***** new *****/
#else                					/***** new *****/
extern void *sbrk __P((ssize_t));
#endif /* osf1v2 */                                     /***** new *****/
extern int ioctl __P((int, unsigned long, char *));
extern pid_t vfork __P((void));
extern int killpg __P((pid_t, int));
extern char *getwd __P((char *));
#endif /* __osf__ && __alpha */

That worked for me. In general, adding checks for OSF1 and correcting the
function prototypes seems to account for a lot of problems.

Pete.


