Chances are that Jaye Mathisen's suggestion will cure the problem.  (I know
that on some RRD40's the dip switch SCSI selector was installed upside down 
-- and I'm not referring to the well-known documentation errors which
indicated that 0 was on and 1 was off -- so you might want to check that.)

Another situation to watch for is bus termination.   Make sure that the
scsi bus is terminated *once*.   Some devices allow for either internal
termination or external termination.   I know of a few devices which, when
internal termination is activated, will honor the external terminator too
if it is connected.   These brain-dead devices result in a doubly-
terminated bus which can produce rather unpleasant results ;-]   You'll
need to check the location of your CD and its termination mechanisms.

If you're convinced that the SCSI id is set correctly on the CD, between 0
and 6, and is unique on the bus; and that the bus is properly terminated;
you might have a firmware problem on the CD.    For example, RRD40's with a
firmware rev of BL25 will not be seen by the >>>SHOW DEVICE command but
will actually work when the system is booted (at least with OpenVMS) -- rev
BL28 is the minimum RRD40 firmware for AXP systems' console to recognize
the device.


	Hope this helps.   Chip Tewksbury.    tewksbury@bu.edu


