In article <0RVPBY5W@math.fu-berlin.de>,
Wolfram Klaus <klaus@omega.physik.fu-berlin.de> wrote:
>  I got the emacs-19.25 distribution, applied the alpha patches as
>indicated and did  a "configure alpha-dec-osf".
>
>  When I now do a *make*, gcc is started with the option "-Olimit 2000"
>and chokes on that. It doesn't know the option -Olimit, ignores it and
>thus cannot find the file 2000.
>
>  I cannot find an entry for this switch either in the man pages or
>the info pages of gcc.
>
>  So: What is this switch supposed to do, why does configure add it to
>the CFLAGS in all the makefiles??

This switch is used by DEC's cc compiler:
  -Olimit num
      Specify the maximum size, in basic blocks, of a routine that will be
      optimized by the global optimizer.  If a routine has more than this
      number of basic blocks it will not be optimized and a message will be
      printed.  A flag specifying that the global optimizer is to be run (-O,
      -O2, or -O3) must also be specified.  The num is assumed to be a
      decimal number.  The default value for num is 500 basic blocks.

Gcc doesn't use this option.

The easiest thing to do would be to re-run configure & tell it 
--with-gcc=no as one of the options.  This will make it use DEC's
compiler.

I've heard of people having problems with the makefile using the DEC
supplied make.  You might want to grab GNU's make & use that if you
have problems later on.

Drew

