Peter da Silva (peter@nmti.com) wrote:
> DECnsr sounds a lot like AMANDA.  AMANDA uses dump to backup each FS, stages
> the dumps on a spool disk, and thence to tape. It manages multiple save sets.
> I'm using it to backup three suns, two Alpha servers, four Alpha workstations,
> and three 486 servers with a single Exabyte 8500.

> And because it uses dump you can pull in a save set and feed it right into
> restore -i.

DECnsr is nothing like dump. For a start it works reliably (When it 
goes, although that can be traced to Legato's product more often than
the DEC end).

Under DECnsr (OK. I'll call it networker or nsr from now on, cause thats what
it really is) you have a client/server model of backups. Somewhere on the
net you run a server. This can be SunOS, RS6000/AUX, or SCO if purchased
from Legato. I believe DEC also do a server for Ultrix and OSF/1. You then
install a copy og the client on the machines you wish to backup. This is
where it becomes interesting. With Legato's product, you run a daemon 
(nsrexecd) which the server talks to, and tells to start backing up. Under
DEC's product, its a full version behind the legato effort (3.x vs 4.02)
and the server uses rsh to start the backups, so you need the server to
be in the root (Or whatever user you use to do backups) .rhosts file. 
(Big security hole for some sites).

OK. Clear as mud now? Good. Basically the server goes out every night
(period between backups can be configured) at a set time and tells the
client to start a backup. The client programs look at their schedule
to see what level backup they should be doing, and then whats in their 
index, and start the appropriate level backup (Backup levels are similar
to dumps, with level0 replaced by full, and the inclusion of a real 
incremental level. You don't have to take the system down to get a good
full/level0 backup).

When restoring you get a text interface or by default a nice graphical
view of your filesystems and the files/dirs (X11).

If you have a layered products CD check out the docs for DECnsr. They 
are basically correct for the Legato product as well.

NOTE!!!

Legato pricing and DEC pricing bear no resemblence to each other. If you
purchase from legato the server is $X(for 10 clients) +$Y(jukebox), and
then you can buy client paks. (You get the client for the same architecture
as the server with the product. ie if you only do RS6000 and you have an
RS6000 server then you don't have to buy client paks.) The number of
clients is done by the server. Not the number of client paks you buy.

There are 3 client paks. 

Pak1 does Unix. Almost all of them with the exception of OSF/1.
Pak2 does PC's. PCNFS (except version 5.x not supported), and SCO, Unixware.
Pak3 does Netware fileservers.

You buy a client pak once only. It then does as many clients as the server
if licenced for in any mix of architectures.

On the other hand, DEC pricing for the server is a mystery to me, and they
charge PER CLIENT for the client paks. If you use Ultrix thats OK, ClientPak1 
includes the ultrix stuff, but under OSF/1 we had to shell out for the
box each time. (Our investment for OSF/1 clients is almost == to what
we paid for all other boxes put together).

