for simple mail forwarding to another address:

in ~/.forward
user@address

for piping to another program:

in ~/.forward (I don't remember off hand if the quotes are suppopse to 
               be there. They are in the system alias file, not sure about
               personal .forward file, but I think they are)
"|path_of_command_to_execute"

Notice that this pipes the message to the command specified.

One such program, called procmail (archie for its' location), lets you
do a of stuff with incomming mail. Such as take all mail of a certain
subject an redirect it to a certain folder; talk all mail from a certain
user a redirect it. Let people know you are on vacation (I think this is
in it) and that you are not just ignoring them.

This is standard on most UNIXes....At least the sendmail ones. I'm not
sure about non-sendmail ones and if it is true for all sendmail ones. I
know osf, bsd, ultrix, brl support it (at least the later 3 did when I
was on them).

Hope this helps some - don't know about mh mail.....

Cheers,
Douglas


