Yes, Digital sells a server for Ultrix and for DEC OSF/1.  Newer
versions only on DEC OSF/1.

> With Legato's product, you run a daemon 
>(nsrexecd) which the server talks to, and tells to start backing up. Under
>DEC's product, its a full version behind the legato effort (3.x vs 4.02)
>and the server uses rsh to start the backups, so you need the server to
>be in the root (Or whatever user you use to do backups) .rhosts file. 
>(Big security hole for some sites).

We're just about to go to field test with a version of NetWorker based
on Legato's 4.0.2 release.  Uses nsrexecd on clients now.

>NOTE!!!
>
>Legato pricing and DEC pricing bear no resemblence to each other.
...
>On the other hand, DEC pricing for the server is a mystery to me, and they
>charge PER CLIENT for the client paks. If you use Ultrix thats OK, ClientPak1 
>includes the ultrix stuff, but under OSF/1 we had to shell out for the
>box each time. (Our investment for OSF/1 clients is almost == to what
>we paid for all other boxes put together).
>

I'm sorry there's confusion.  There is not really that much difference
between the DEC pricing policy and Legato's.  Here's DEC's:

You pay $ for a NetWorker server license.  This lets you run the
server software and backup the server itself.

If you have a jukebox, you pay $ for a jukebox license.  Cost based
on the capacity of the jukebox.

You pay ($ X number of clients) for client licenses.  These are
registered on the server.  Here's where DEC differs from Legato.  Say
you want to backup 10 clients.  From Legato, you would buy a server
license with a capacity of 10.  From DEC you would buy a server
license, plus 10 client licenses.  You do NOT buy 10 boxes from DEC!

DEC's packaging is similar to Legato's too.  We have a SERVER kit,
which also includes all DEC client software (Ultrix, OSF/1, PATHWORKS
PC).  For non-DEC clients, we just sell Legato's client pak kits,
so you buy

    Client pack I for UNIX clients
    Client pack II for xxx
    Client pack III for xxx

You only have to buy ONE of these to cover each type of client.
If you only have UNIX clients, you only buy one Client pack I kit.

Perhaps the confusion is the use of the word "pack" or "PAK".  In
DEC terminology, a "PAK" is a "Product Authorization Key" (license).
In Legato terminology, a "pack" (?"PAK"?) is a PAcKage (box).
Different concepts.

Sorry I don't know the actual prices.  I'm just a software engineer
working on the product.


