>While working recently on some issues relevant to the Intel Paragon
>operating system (a version of OSF/1), the acronym NORMA cropped up.
>I didn't think about it for a long time, but my curiosity has reminded
>me that I never found out what I wanted to know about the term.  I
>believe it stands for NO Remote Memory Access, but that doesn't really
>define the term for me.  I think it refers to the specific set of Mach
>port rights or something selected for the version of OSF/1 on which
>the Paragon OS is based.  So did the term originate with OSF?  I did
>not think this was an Intel-specific term, I believe I've seen it in
>other circumstances.  No mention of it in the "Design of OSF/1" book.

Your definition is correct.  The term NORMA was coined to refer to a
class of machine architecture such as the Intel Paragon, a
multicomputer which has no global shared memory available between
nodes.  Sort of a follow-on to UMA, NUMA, and now NORMA.

Joe Barrera, as part of his early doctoral work, extended Mach IPC to
be distributed while transparently maintaining local port semantics.
This work was called NORMA IPC (...a real misnomer, if you think about
it).  For several reasons, this work provided both correctness and
performance the netmsg server could never do.

OSF picked up this work some time ago and has continued to
develop it and do research in this space.  A new implementation,
dubbed DIPC (Distributed IPC - way better name, eh?) is currently
under way.

NORMA was originally developed for the Intel Hypercube and was
successfully put onto the successor, the Intel Paragon.  Intel worked
closely with OSF and Locus to produce the Paragon operating system, an
implementation of OSF/1 AD - a fully distributed OS based on Mach 3.0
with NORMA extensions.  Finally, they added their own parallel
enhancements.

There are papers on NORMA available via anonymous ftp on riftp
(130.105.99.2), /pub/os_coll_papers, along with a bunch of stuff on
OSF/1 AD.


