In article <Jay_Masino.57.001233DA@ccmail.gsfc.nasa.gov>, Jay_Masino@ccmail.gsfc.nasa.gov (Jay Masino) writes:
- I'd like to use a single tape to do my incrementals (using OSF/1's dump) and 
- would like to know what happens if I just continue to do dumps to the same 
- tape without allowing a rewind.

You can do WITH a rewind. It also makes for faster access to the newest version of
a file. 

If you cannot afford the risk of the system going down while you make your 
incremental dump (and overwriting the previous one), then you can certainly 
not afford the risk of the tape falling into a honeypot or being wiped by
a rogue command involving tape write.

The notion that part of a tape is "safe" while other bits are being written
is fanciful. View a tape as useless until it has been written and read back
(for example to extract a list of files), extracted from the machine and stored
in a reasonably fire, and water and dustproof way. 

Conversely, if you have a valuable tape (containing important data), do you 
really want to entrust that to unreliable mechanical devices like tapedrives
which will just chew it up ?

