I've had some odd experiences with the combination of the above.

As a preface, I'll say we've been running ADVFS on Quantum LPS 540, 
Seagate ST11200N, and Segate ST42100 drives with no problems for
over a now, so we decided to try it out on our DEC DSP3160S.

At first, it seemed to work OK, but then the system started to "hang up"
when you did some I/O intensive operation on the ADVFS file system
built on the DSP3160S.  You'd start something like a
	find . -name core -exec rm {} \;
and in about 15 minutes the system would just stop for 5 minutes or so.

Then we rebooted the machine -- complete disaster!  When the system
first tried to access the ADVFS file system on the DSP3160S, you'd
get screens of error messages about "bus reset" and even "chip anomaly."
We had to reboot in single-user mode and remove the DSP3160S file system
reference from /etc/fstab.  However, once we rebooted we could
manually mount the DSP3160S file system without problem.

At this time, we do seem to have a "solution" to the problem.
We edited /sys/data/cam_data.c to add an entry to the DSP3160S
that is similar to the entry for the RZ27.  (I guessed that as
those Digital disks are the same size, that they must behave
simularly.)  The entry looks like:
{"DEC     DSP3160S", 16, DEV_RZxx, (ALL_DTYPE_DIRECT << DTYPE_SHFT) | SZ_HARD_DISK,
  (struct pt_info *)ccmn_rzxx_sizes, 512, DEC_MAX_REC, NO_DENS_TAB, NO_MODE_TAB,
  SZ_BBR ,
  NO_OPT_CMDS, SZ_READY_DEF, 74, DD_REQSNS_VAL | DD_INQ_VAL,
  36, 64
},
Now we are able to boot the machine OK with the ADVFS file system
on the DSP3160S listed in /etc/fstab.  Once since then, the disk did
seem to hang up, but that had happened a couple of times in the
past when we were running UFS on the DSP3160S.  Maybe I still don't 
have the right entry in cam_data.c.


