Dear Net,

The ghostscript (2.6.1) on my Alpha (OSF1/V1.2)
cannot display .ps files created by TeX/LaTeX/dvips
that use the computer modern fonts.
Other workstations correctly display these .ps files.
Ghostscript fonts used in TeX/LaTeX/dvips will display normally.

My gs was built with the four patches from GNU,
the patch from Matt Reeve, and the patch to std.h,
lines 143-153 (+ #ifdef __OSF__<cr>+ typedef unsigned int ushort;).
It compiles and runs without error messages,
producing normal output with non-cm-font input.

This is a serious problem, because there appears to be
no other way to convert .ps files to .ppm files
in a batch environment.  Latex2html uses gs to convert
math text such as $^2$ to images.  Without a working
gs, latex2html cannot be used effectively on the Alpha.
The ps viewer supplied by DEC, psview/xpsview, can do
the conversion, but not, as I understand it, in batch mode,
and latex2html can generate hundreds of little .ps files
that require conversion.
Latex2html ignores the latex \newfont command, so ghostscript
fonts cannot be used in these documents. 

If you think that you have a fully functional ghostview,
try to display the .ps file included below, or recreate
it from the following short LaTeX document ($testit.tex).
If it works, please post your additional patches to this group.
If you have an alternative way to convert .ps files
to .ppm files, please let me know, and post to this group.

Thanks in advance,
Bob Williams, Dept. of Biochem., USUHS, bob@bob.usuf2.usuhs.nnmc.navy.mil 

