>>>>> "chris" == Christopher Samuel <chris@rivers.dra.hmg.gb> writes:

    chris> In article <2sl08s$1hl@coranto.ucs.mun.ca> of
    chris> comp.unix.osf.osf1, andrew@bransle (Andrew Draskoy) doodled:

    >> The method can be changed by removing the symbolic link
    >> "/sbin/swapdefault".

Terminology notes:
The method of swap allocation with the /sbin/swapdefault symlink in
place is reffered to "immediate" mode.
The other method (without the symlink there) is "deferred" mode.
Immediate mode is the default configuration (atleast on 1.3 systems).
In immediate mode, the kernel attempts to determine and pre-allocate the
amount of space required for each process and to play a "keep-ahead"
kind of game.
In deferred mode, swap space is allocated dynamically based on the needs
of processes.  It is possible to run out of free pages in this mode.  In
this case, the system will kill a process to free a page(s).

    chris> I suspect this also caused a couple of panic's on our OSF/1
    chris> 2.0 Alpha here. From reading the runes I guess the sequence
    chris> of events to be something along the lines of:

    chris> Someone tries to start a process Kernel tries to pre-allocate
    chris> swap for it Kernel finds no swap available to pre-allocate
    chris> Kernel tries to kill process Whilst signalling the process
    chris> the kernel tries to allocate some memory The alloc fails The
    chris> kernel panic's

This does not jive.  In immediate mode, as I understand the DEC
documentation, the system will not randomly kill processes.  This only
happens in deffered mode.

    chris> Since I've turned off DECs braindead swap preallocation we've
    chris> not had a recurrence of it.

This is interesting.  We had to turn off immediate mode because we
did not have swap >= phys. RAM.  You cannot get total use out of your
RAM in immediate mode without enough swap space to match it (braindead).
Now that we have ~2xRAM for swap space, I should probably switch back to
deferred just to avoid the risk of randomly killing processes.

Comments?  Have I got it all jumbled?

